[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Making the rules executable

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP
TITLE: The rules become executable in five milestones, plumbing before migration
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The fifth milestone is verified, or a milestone's review changes the order or
the number of milestones.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The rules of the design system become executable in five milestones, in this
order: one, the grammar generated from a typed Nix option surface with a
validation gate at commit and in CI; two, a CLI that is the only writer of sdoc
files, with direct edits denied; three, the behaviour model -- states, moves,
gates, triggers, flows -- defined in Nix and rendered for review; four, that
behaviour wired into the CLI and into checkpoints at command, commit and merge
time, with strictdoc's own hook as the backstop; five, this system's own
semantics -- fingerprints, signing, the ripple, DEPTH -- migrated onto that
plumbing.

Every milestone is consumer-facing, so each ends in two operator reviews: the
option surface or verb list is reviewed before the milestone is implemented,
and the implementation is reviewed before the next milestone starts.
Milestones one and two are separate slices and separate sessions. Migration
waits for the plumbing; the operator may integrate an earlier milestone sooner.

One implementing session per milestone, taking the slice UID. The planning
session that produced these nodes stays open, to re-slot a milestone whose
timing was wrong.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The activity began because a model produces sdoc in incorrect formats, nothing
validates a write when it happens, and the operator could not reason about the
node lifecycle from prose. Milestones one and two end the first two problems
without waiting on any lifecycle decision; milestone three is where the
lifecycle becomes something that can be looked at; only after four does a rule
run rather than get read, which is what INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE asks for.
Ordering migration last keeps the instance's open decisions -- who signs,
whether DEPTH may regress, agent identity -- from blocking the plumbing.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The evidence behind every milestone is the SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES assessment
and the fold-in review of two outside research documents that followed it,
both held in the operator's working area outside the repository.

A step zero precedes milestone one: SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY, the overlay that
keeps strictdoc on the latest release. Not a milestone -- it has no consumer
surface to review -- but every milestone runs on it.
<<<

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY
TITLE: Step zero -- an overlay that keeps strictdoc on the latest release
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Add a first-party overlay for strictdoc that tracks upstream's latest GitHub
release, so every session, check and devShell in this repository runs the
current version and the nixpkgs version stops being a topic. Shape: a thin
override of the nixpkgs derivation carrying our version and source from a
sidecar, with the two dependency adjustments the current release needs -- a
newer reqif and a relaxed pygments pin -- registered like the other tracked
packages: an update-targets row on the release-tracking contract, a
cache-hit-parity row, and the strictdoc-using flake checks and the devShell
switched to the overlay package. No patches; if one is ever needed it applies
with fuzz disabled.

No operator review before implementation: this is plumbing, not a consumer
surface. Reviewed after: `strictdoc version` in the devShell and in the checks
reports the latest release, and the update sweep bumps it on its own.

Tracking main instead of releases is the operator's call per package, made on
release cadence and how stable main is; strictdoc ships roughly three releases
a month, so release tracking is the default here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Standing rule from the operator: any package used seriously gets an overlay
that tracks the latest release or main -- update cadence is never a
consideration, and the version in nixpkgs is never an input. Several sessions
noted the version gap and none built the overlay; this node exists so the next
session builds it rather than noting it again. Everything after it -- the
grammar gate, the CLI, the behaviour model -- is measured against the latest
release and should run on it.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
NOTES: >>>
Precedents to follow: the thin Python override in overlays/git-tools/
git-revise.nix, the release-tracking update-targets row for beads, the
cache-hit-parity registry, and overlays/generic/tree-sitter-strictdoc.nix for
the fuzz-free patch flags should a patch ever appear. Group: dev-tools, the
same class as beads.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP
  ROLE: Governed_By
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES
  ROLE: Proven_By

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX
TITLE: Generate the grammar from a typed Nix option surface, and gate it
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Define the grammar as a typed Nix option tree: one ordered master list of
fields, each node type selecting the fields it carries and specialising a
field where it must (STATUS differs between DECISION and SPIKE); relations and
prefixes per type; no untyped option anywhere. Generate docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra
from it, byte-identical to today's, plus a committed sidecar naming the
contract-bearing fields per type, and keep the @repo alias resolving. Commit
the generated files and add a drift check, so a stale grammar is a red check.
Add the validation gate: a clean-directory strictdoc export over the whole
corpus at commit time and in CI, because some grammar defects only surface
through documents.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the option tree
for node types and fields. Reviewed after: the regenerated grammar as a no-op
diff, and the gate firing on a deliberately broken fixture.

Not included: behaviour, transitions, ownership rules, and the contract-field
selection itself -- this delivers the surface, not the values.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Proven in the spike: a typed surface regenerates the grammar byte-for-byte in
0.03 s and rejects thirteen of thirteen bad declarations once the merged value
is forced, and strictdoc rejects six of six injected grammar defects. The gate
needs nothing but the strictdoc package. Two rules carried from the
measurement: cross-field checks live in one apply that forces evaluation,
because addCheck never runs on a submodule-typed option; and the corpus export
is the second gate.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Carry the full native grammar surface -- HUMAN_TITLE, IS_COMPOSITE,
VIEW_STYLE, Child relations, File roles, a three-state PREFIX -- rather than
excluding them as design choices. One shape question the review left open is
brought as the first review item: one global field order, or per-element
order (strictdoc validates order per element and accepts both). Update the
sdoc skill wherever the generated grammar changes its instructions.
<<<
