[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-SDOC-CLI
TITLE: A CLI that is the only writer of sdoc files
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Build the command-line tool through which every node is created and changed:
create, set, relate and move, with flags derived from the generated grammar
(fields, choices, relation roles); UIDs minted through strictdoc's own hook;
required fields filled from arguments so a TBD never lands; every write
validated against the whole graph before the file is replaced; formatting
applied by the same code path. Deny direct edits of sdoc and grammar files at
the tool-call layer and point the model at the CLI. No lifecycle verbs yet:
this milestone is CRUD.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the verb list
and their flags. Reviewed after: a session that authors nodes through the CLI
only, and a direct edit being refused.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The tool takes the model's hands off the files, which is the root failure:
incorrect formats and unvalidated writes. Measured: creating a node in-process
costs 0.35 s, validating the prospective state 0.3 s, and strictdoc's node
model writes any field in grammar order -- nothing here waits on the behaviour
model.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
NOTES: >>>
Build on strictdoc's own machinery rather than re-implementing writes: manage
new for scaffolding, set_field_value for fields, the writer for formatting.
The cache directory must live outside the checkout for worktrees. Validating
the whole graph per command is affordable warm -- about a second on this
corpus, two at five thousand nodes -- only when the candidate copy sits at a
stable path sharing one output directory; a fresh temporary path is cold.
<<<
