[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL
TITLE: Define the behaviour model in Nix and render it for review
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Define, as a typed Nix option tree layered over the grammar: the lifecycle of
each node type -- its states over grammar fields, the legal moves, who may
make each, what each writes; triggers -- a command, an outside event such as
landing in main, a timer, another move finishing; gates and invariants, each
declaring what it needs to see so that the places it can run are derived;
relation contracts -- which node types a role may point at, cycles,
propagation; and flows as ordered references to moves. Cross-check every value
against the grammar at evaluation. From one evaluation emit a machine-readable
model for the CLI and the validators, a state diagram per node type, a diagram
per flow, a table of moves against gates, a table of gates against where they
can run, and a report of what is still only prose.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the option tree
and the rendered diagrams -- this is where the node lifecycle becomes
something to look at rather than guess at. Three shape questions are open and
are brought one at a time before the surface is fixed: whether state is one
machine per field or one machine over the tuple of fields; whether the ripple
is derived from declared read and write scopes or from the contract-field
flag; whether a flow is a list of move references or a step-by-step script.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Every part of this was prototyped in the spike and its review: both state
shapes, four representations of gate placement that all derive the same
result -- including an empty run set for the authorship guard -- flows that
drift when they restate moves, and eight rendered views from one evaluation.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Vocabulary adopted from the outside research: a command is a request and may
be refused; an event is an accepted fact; a milestone is an achieved fact that
can go stale; a gate's evidence records what it depended on. The instance's
signed edge is a milestone with evidence rather than a node type of its own --
contested in review, revisit when the surface is fixed.
<<<
